# `ProblemBuilder::nl_ineq`

[Back to `optim::ProblemBuilder`](../ProblemBuilder.md)

The short name of `nonlinear_inequality`.

## Signature

```cpp
template <typename Fn> optim::ProblemBuilder &optim::ProblemBuilder::nl_ineq(Fn &&fn);
```

## What It Does

Identical to [`nonlinear_inequality`](nonlinear_inequality.md): `fn` returns `g(x)` and the constraint is `g(x) ≤ 0`.

## Parameters

`Fn &&fn`
: Returns `g(x)`.

## Returns

The same builder, for chaining.

## Practical Examples

### Example 1

The unit-disc problem with the short name.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

using namespace pluss::tils;

namespace optim = pluss::subsp::math::optim;
using Pack = optim::ParameterPack<pluss::Series>;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(4));
  const pluss::Series target = pluss::series::make({1.0, 2.0});

  const optim::OptimaState result = optim::optima(optim::problem().objective([&](const Pack &p) { return (p.at("x") - target).square().sum(); }).nl_ineq([](const Pack &p) { return p.at("x").square().sum() - 1.0; }), pluss::series::make({0.0, 0.0}));
  out.writeln("x =", result.params.at("x").items<double>());
}
```

### Example 2

A halfspace written as a nonlinear row: `x₀ + x₁ ≤ 2`.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

using namespace pluss::tils;

namespace optim = pluss::subsp::math::optim;
using Pack = optim::ParameterPack<pluss::Series>;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(4));
  const pluss::Series target = pluss::series::make({1.0, 2.0});

  const optim::OptimaState result = optim::optima(optim::problem().objective([&](const Pack &p) { return (p.at("x") - target).square().sum(); }).nl_ineq([](const Pack &p) { return p.at("x").sum() - 2.0; }), pluss::series::make({0.0, 0.0}));
  out.writeln("x =", result.params.at("x").items<double>());
}
```

### Example 3

Two inequalities cannot be chained; the second call replaces the first. Combine them into one callback that returns both residuals, here `x₀ + x₁ ≤ 2` and `x₀ ≤ 0.25`.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

using namespace pluss::tils;

namespace optim = pluss::subsp::math::optim;
using Pack = optim::ParameterPack<pluss::Series>;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(4));
  const pluss::Series target = pluss::series::make({1.0, 2.0});

  const optim::OptimaState result = optim::optima(optim::problem().objective([&](const Pack &p) { return (p.at("x") - target).square().sum(); }).nl_ineq([](const Pack &p) {
    const pluss::Series rows = pluss::series::make({{1.0, 1.0}, {1.0, 0.0}});
    return rows.matmul(p.at("x").reshape({2, 1})).reshape({2}) - pluss::series::make({2.0, 0.25});
  }), pluss::series::make({0.0, 0.0}));
  out.writeln("x =", result.params.at("x").items<double>());
}
```

